WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.871
Welcome to Module 9 of the
Data Platform and SQL Server

2
00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:07.250
Identity learning path:
closure, drift and evidence.

3
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.929
It teaches Runbook R nine,
continuous monitoring and closure,

4
00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:15.462
with Chapter 9 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:15.462 --> 00:00:20.196
You'll project an audit run onto the five
closure conditions, see which fields the

6
00:00:20.196 --> 00:00:24.788
audit can't settle by itself, and compare
two runs so a regression becomes a task.

7
00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:27.251
Take this module after Module 8.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.387
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:32.387 --> 00:00:36.074
An instance brought to the
Entra-exclusive posture can slide back.

10
00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:38.831
An administrator can
switch it to mixed mode,

11
00:00:38.831 --> 00:00:41.513
create a SQL login, or
add a Windows login.

12
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:43.812
So closure isn't a one-time action.

13
00:00:43.812 --> 00:00:46.776
It's a record that keeps coming
back clean on a schedule,

14
00:00:46.776 --> 00:00:48.488
and drift is the run that doesn't.

15
00:00:48.488 --> 00:00:52.698
In this module you project an audit
run onto the five closure conditions,

16
00:00:52.698 --> 00:00:55.504
see which fields the audit
can't settle by itself,

17
00:00:55.504 --> 00:00:58.751
and compare two runs so a
regression becomes a task.

18
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.319
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

19
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.900
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

20
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.131
You'll name the five
closure conditions and

21
00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:12.475
the fields the closure
record shows for each.

22
00:01:12.475 --> 00:01:15.356
You'll explain why two
fields are always external,

23
00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:19.712
and why a record with nothing not met is
ready for the assessor, never closed.

24
00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.403
You'll run the kit's closure record
and drift comparison on the day-one

25
00:01:23.403 --> 00:01:26.900
and day-thirty fixtures, with and
without the exception registry.

26
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.268
And you'll say what the
drift check catches,

27
00:01:29.268 --> 00:01:32.650
and why Arc going offline needs
an Arc snapshot with each run.

28
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.112
Before you start, finish Module 8.

29
00:01:35.112 --> 00:01:40.530
The closure record reads the audit from
Module 2 and the Arc snapshot from Module 3.

30
00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:47.105
Unit 2 is the five closure conditions.

31
00:01:47.105 --> 00:01:50.757
The data platform book, Data
Platform: SQL Server Identity,

32
00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:54.531
defines the transformation as
complete when five conditions

33
00:01:54.531 --> 00:01:57.580
hold at the same time on
every production instance.

34
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.939
One: no usable Windows-backed principal,

35
00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:03.442
other than the break-glass
group in the exception registry.

36
00:02:03.442 --> 00:02:06.217
Two: SQL Authentication eliminated.

37
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:11.154
No SQL logins outside the registry,
the S A account disabled and renamed,

38
00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:14.736
and SQL logins refused,
which on SQL Server 2025

39
00:02:14.736 --> 00:02:18.318
also needs is external
authentication only set to one.

40
00:02:18.318 --> 00:02:22.529
Three: Entra authentication
observed working, and nothing else:

41
00:02:22.529 --> 00:02:27.933
every usable login is an Entra type, the
sign-in logs and SQL Server Audit show it,

42
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:31.430
and there are no remote N T
L M or Kerberos connections.

43
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.587
Four: a cloud identity for the engine:
the Arc agent connected, the SQL extension

44
00:02:36.587 --> 00:02:41.592
deployed, and Entra configured, which
failover cluster instances don't support.

45
00:02:41.592 --> 00:02:43.992
Five: M F A for every human.

46
00:02:43.992 --> 00:02:46.118
None of them is a single engine setting.

47
00:02:46.118 --> 00:02:50.120
Login mode one proves only
that SQL logins are refused.

48
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.786
The kit's closure record shows
eleven fields per instance,

49
00:02:57.786 --> 00:03:00.282
and each one is met, not met, or external.

50
00:03:00.282 --> 00:03:03.171
Fields the audit can't
see are external joins:

51
00:03:03.171 --> 00:03:07.620
Entra sign-ins and audit, and M F A
for every human, are always external.

52
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.558
The record points at those
logs and doesn't copy them.

53
00:03:10.558 --> 00:03:14.708
Condition four comes from the Arc
snapshot, and is external without it.

54
00:03:14.708 --> 00:03:17.160
The exception registry names a login,

55
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.432
an instance or a star for every
instance, an approver and an expiry.

56
00:03:21.432 --> 00:03:24.971
The record is narrower than the
book's field set in two places,

57
00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:29.642
so read those by hand: condition three
checks login types but doesn't join them to

58
00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:33.322
the Entra inventory, and the
protocol field reads one snapshot,

59
00:03:33.322 --> 00:03:35.233
not the whole validation period.

60
00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:38.453
An instance with nothing not
met is ready for the assessor,

61
00:03:38.453 --> 00:03:39.996
with the external evidence.

62
00:03:39.996 --> 00:03:41.345
It's never closed.

63
00:03:41.345 --> 00:03:44.045
The book's Chapter 21,
What Done Looks Like,

64
00:03:44.045 --> 00:03:49.604
names six live evidence sets that close
the transformation: the compliance records,

65
00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:53.654
the Entra sign-in logs, the N T L
M telemetry, the S A status report,

66
00:03:53.654 --> 00:03:57.860
the Conditional Access report
and the exception registry.

67
00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.323
Unit 3 is drift.

68
00:04:03.323 --> 00:04:06.024
A drift event is a
regression between two runs,

69
00:04:06.024 --> 00:04:10.660
and the book treats each one as a compliance
violation, not an advisory signal.

70
00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.967
The kit's comparison returns a row
for a new usable Windows or SQL login,

71
00:04:15.967 --> 00:04:21.447
an Entra login removed, S A re-enabled,
SQL authentication accepted again,

72
00:04:21.447 --> 00:04:25.623
remote N T L M back after none,
or an instance not seen before.

73
00:04:25.623 --> 00:04:29.897
It applies the exception registry, so
the break-glass group isn't drift.

74
00:04:29.897 --> 00:04:34.251
The Arc agent going offline changes
nothing in the audit, so the comparison

75
00:04:34.251 --> 00:04:38.184
reports Arc regressed only when you
give it both runs' Arc snapshots.

76
00:04:38.184 --> 00:04:41.935
And a mode changed in the registry
but not yet restarted is invisible,

77
00:04:41.935 --> 00:04:44.172
because the audit reads the running mode.

78
00:04:44.172 --> 00:04:47.659
Runbook R nine adds a registry
check of login mode for that.

79
00:04:47.659 --> 00:04:50.548
Each row is a finding: open a task for it.

80
00:04:50.548 --> 00:04:55.070
The workbook Building Ticket-to-Machine
turns a drift row into a typed task.

81
00:04:58.870 --> 00:04:59.757
Try it.

82
00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:03.170
Lab 1 runs on the kit's
fictional estate, with no tenant.

83
00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.666
Open PowerShell 7, go to the kit's folder,

84
00:05:05.666 --> 00:05:09.645
and make this run's results folder,
named for the time you start.

85
00:05:09.645 --> 00:05:13.095
Step one projects day thirty
onto the closure conditions.

86
00:05:13.095 --> 00:05:18.140
Sequel P R D oh one, migrated in Chapter
4, has nine fields met, none not met,

87
00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:23.445
and two external, so it's ready for the
assessor, with the external evidence.

88
00:05:23.445 --> 00:05:24.995
The other three are open.

89
00:05:24.995 --> 00:05:30.834
Sequel P R D oh six fails SQL logins refused,
although is integrated security only

90
00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:36.757
is one, because it runs SQL Server 2025 and
is external authentication only is zero.

91
00:05:36.757 --> 00:05:40.469
Sequel F C I oh four is a
failover cluster instance.

92
00:05:40.469 --> 00:05:43.207
It can't take Entra
authentication as built,

93
00:05:43.207 --> 00:05:46.020
so it needs a re-platform
or a dated exception.

94
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.580
The last line names the evidence:
C A seven, C M six and A C two.

95
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.717
Step two reads sequel P R
D oh one's whole record.

96
00:05:58.717 --> 00:06:01.068
Every field the audit can settle is met.

97
00:06:01.068 --> 00:06:05.220
The two external rows are condition
three's Entra sign-ins and audit,

98
00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.667
and condition five's
M F A for every human.

99
00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:11.054
They're evidence sets two and
five of the book's Chapter 21:

100
00:06:11.054 --> 00:06:14.442
the Entra sign-in logs and
the Conditional Access report.

101
00:06:14.442 --> 00:06:17.055
Step three reads the exception registry.

102
00:06:17.055 --> 00:06:20.212
The break-glass group is
excepted on every instance,

103
00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:23.443
with no expiry, approved by
the authorizing official.

104
00:06:23.443 --> 00:06:27.919
The vendor login on sequel
F C I oh four has a date:

105
00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:31.320
June 30, 2027, approved by the I S S O.

106
00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.620
Step four shows what the registry does.

107
00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.464
It builds the same instance's
record with no exception registry

108
00:06:41.464 --> 00:06:44.770
and no Arc snapshot, and lists
every field that isn't met.

109
00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:49.375
Without the registry, sequel P R D oh
one's enabled break-glass group fails

110
00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:54.133
conditions one and three: it's a usable
Windows login, and it isn't an Entra one.

111
00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:58.758
Without the Arc snapshot, all three
fields of condition four become external.

112
00:06:58.758 --> 00:07:00.733
Nothing about the instance changed.

113
00:07:00.733 --> 00:07:02.870
Only what the record was given.

114
00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:11.270
Step five compares day one with day thirty,
with the exception registry applied.

115
00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:14.907
One row comes back: a
Windows user, contoso J doe,

116
00:07:14.907 --> 00:07:18.595
added to the sys-admin role
on sequel P R D oh six.

117
00:07:18.595 --> 00:07:23.070
That's a new legacy login, and a
compliance finding: open a task for it.

118
00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.658
The break-glass group is in both runs
and in the registry, so it isn't drift.

119
00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:29.696
Notice what this run wasn't given:

120
00:07:29.696 --> 00:07:33.250
either run's Arc snapshot, so
it doesn't read Arc at all.

121
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.588
Step six opens the drift manifest.

122
00:07:39.588 --> 00:07:45.913
It records the step, drift; its mode,
read-only; the snapshot's date; the controls,

123
00:07:45.913 --> 00:07:49.354
C A seven and C M six; the
K S Is to line up with;

124
00:07:49.354 --> 00:07:53.099
and the S H A two fifty-six
of each file it wrote.

125
00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:57.062
Step seven runs this chapter's
tests: eight pass, none fail.

126
00:07:57.062 --> 00:07:59.738
Step eight lists your
runs, one line per run.

127
00:07:59.738 --> 00:08:01.276
There's nothing to clean up.

128
00:08:01.276 --> 00:08:03.332
The kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

129
00:08:03.332 --> 00:08:05.800
and this run's results
stay in the run folder.

130
00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.713
Check your work: you can say why sequel P
R D oh one is ready for the assessor but

131
00:08:10.713 --> 00:08:14.611
not closed, why sequel P R D oh
six fails SQL logins refused,

132
00:08:14.611 --> 00:08:19.512
and why the new sys-admin login is
drift while the break-glass group isn't.

133
00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.885
Lab 2 schedules the same runs
against one test instance.

134
00:08:26.885 --> 00:08:28.635
It wasn't run by the authors.

135
00:08:28.635 --> 00:08:32.118
You need a test job host and a
test instance, never production,

136
00:08:32.118 --> 00:08:34.835
and a group managed service
account on the host.

137
00:08:34.835 --> 00:08:39.312
Its Windows login on the instance holds
the read rights the audit exporter states,

138
00:08:39.312 --> 00:08:41.683
never sys-admin, and it's
recorded in the test

139
00:08:41.683 --> 00:08:44.448
exception registry with an
approver and an expiry.

140
00:08:44.448 --> 00:08:47.146
Write a run script that
takes one audit snapshot,

141
00:08:47.146 --> 00:08:51.260
projects it onto the closure record,
and compares it with the previous run.

142
00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.698
Run it once by hand, then schedule it weekly
as the group managed service account.

143
00:08:55.698 --> 00:08:57.209
The runs are read-only.

144
00:08:57.209 --> 00:09:01.603
For condition four, someone with Reader
in commercial Azure exports an Arc

145
00:09:01.603 --> 00:09:05.997
snapshot before each run, because the
service account can't sign in to Azure.

146
00:09:05.997 --> 00:09:09.161
Step five backs it out by
removing the scheduled task.

147
00:09:09.161 --> 00:09:12.730
The run folders stay as
evidence, inside your boundary.

148
00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.130
The federal requirement.

149
00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:25.585
Chapter 9 maps the closure record to
three NIST SP 800-53 controls: C A seven,

150
00:09:25.585 --> 00:09:29.577
continuous monitoring; C M six,
the authentication settings,

151
00:09:29.577 --> 00:09:32.930
read every run; and A C two,
which accounts can sign in.

152
00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:36.792
It maps the drift comparison
to C A seven and C M six.

153
00:09:36.792 --> 00:09:40.836
Keep the closure file and its manifest
per instance per run, the exception

154
00:09:40.836 --> 00:09:45.343
registry with its approvers, and the
external evidence each external field names.

155
00:09:45.343 --> 00:09:50.205
For drift, keep the drift file, its
manifest, and the task opened for each row.

156
00:09:50.205 --> 00:09:54.352
Each manifest records the
step, its controls and K S Is,

157
00:09:54.352 --> 00:09:57.180
and the S H A two fifty-six of each file.

158
00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:01.693
Runbook R nine lists the evidence for the
evidenced and operated control states.

159
00:10:01.693 --> 00:10:05.890
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers:

160
00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:09.705
your cloud providers report
against them, the agency doesn't.

161
00:10:09.705 --> 00:10:11.702
The records support these controls;

162
00:10:11.702 --> 00:10:15.150
the assessor decides whether
the closure posture holds.

163
00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.000
Check yourself with two questions.

164
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.795
First: sequel P R D oh one's
day-thirty record has nine fields met,

165
00:10:25.795 --> 00:10:28.075
none not met, and two external.

166
00:10:28.075 --> 00:10:29.375
What does the kit call it?

167
00:10:29.375 --> 00:10:33.763
The answer is b: ready for the
assessor, with the external evidence.

168
00:10:33.763 --> 00:10:38.049
The two external fields still need their
evidence, and the assessor decides.

169
00:10:38.049 --> 00:10:43.250
Second: sequel P R D oh six has is
integrated security only set to one,

170
00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:46.087
yet SQL logins refused is not met.

171
00:10:46.087 --> 00:10:46.801
Why?

172
00:10:46.801 --> 00:10:49.949
The answer is a: it runs SQL Server 2025,

173
00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:53.375
and is external
authentication only is zero.

174
00:10:53.375 --> 00:10:58.030
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

175
00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:02.855
To sum up.

176
00:11:02.855 --> 00:11:06.223
You projected an audit run onto
the five closure conditions,

177
00:11:06.223 --> 00:11:09.436
saw which fields wait on
evidence from outside the audit,

178
00:11:09.436 --> 00:11:12.854
saw what the exception registry
and the Arc snapshot change,

179
00:11:12.854 --> 00:11:15.930
and compared two runs so a
regression became a finding.

180
00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:19.666
Closure is the record that keeps
coming back clean on a schedule,

181
00:11:19.666 --> 00:11:21.217
and each drift row is a task.

182
00:11:21.217 --> 00:11:24.293
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 9 checklist.

183
00:11:24.293 --> 00:11:27.910
Next, Module 10: deploying
to test and production.
